   {
    "t": 16.25,
    "type": "done",
    "data": {
     "answer": "ADR 0010 kept Chroma as the vector store when it added hybrid retrieval and reranking. The decision did not replace Chroma with a store that handles keyword search. Instead, the project keeps Chroma for vector search and runs BM25 keyword search in Python over the same chunks, built in memory at start-up. Vector and keyword results are merged with Reciprocal Rank Fusion, and the top 10 candidates are reranked by a cross-encoder (`Xenova/ms-marco-MiniLM-L-6-v2` via fastembed). (0010-hybrid-retrieval-with-reranking.md, section \"Decision\")\n\nThe retained vector store is the original choice from ADR 0001, which ADR 0010 explicitly revisits and keeps. (0001-chroma-as-vector-store.md, section \"0001. Chroma as the vector store\"; 0010-hybrid-retrieval-with-reranking.md, section \"0010. Cleaner parsing, hybrid retrieval and reranking; keep Chroma\")\n\nThe excerpts I retrieved state the decision but do not spell out the full reasoning for keeping Chroma, such as the alternatives considered or the specific cost of switching. For that, read the \"Context\" and \"Alternatives\" sections of ADR 0010 directly. One related design choice is also visible: ADR 0010 kept the chunking unchanged (1000 characters, 150 overlap) so that the effect of the retrieval changes could be measured on its own. That is a useful pattern when you change one part of a pipeline at a time.",
     "sources": [
      "0001-chroma-as-vector-store.md, section \"0001. Chroma as the vector store\"",
      "0010-hybrid-retrieval-with-reranking.md, section \"0010. Cleaner parsing, hybrid retrieval and reranking; keep Chroma\"",
      "0010-hybrid-retrieval-with-reranking.md, section \"Decision\""
     ],
     "guard": "ok"
    }
   }
  ],
